From e40ca1724e05508b43349986622572bc249228a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Beany <24502088+beanyforever@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 22:56:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Caches serve no copy older than the file it copies

SiYuan caches pages, blocks' and pages' attributes, and databases in
ristretto, and an entry didn't know which version of its file it
copied, so a cache could serve a copy older than its file, and the next
edit could save that copy over the file:

- ristretto takes in a key it doesn't hold yet later, on its own
  goroutine, and turns away a second put made before the first is taken
  in, so of two quick puts of a new block's attributes the first stayed:
  "+ New" in a table left the page it made without custom-avs about 1
  time in 3 to 6;
- a read that missed a cache cached what it read even if a save landed
  meanwhile, and fixTreeJSONData wrote its fixed copy back over that save;
- a page moved to another notebook and back loaded as it was when it
  left;
- a restored page kept its blocks' old attributes, and a read of a
  database's history copy cached it as the live database;
- a job put the attributes it changed in the cache before its commit
  wrote the page, and left them there if it failed.

Each page id and database id now has a version, drawn from one clock
that only rises (cache/fill.go). WriteTree, WriteTreeIfUnchanged,
fixTreeJSONData's write-back and saveAttributeView raise it at both ends
of their write, through BeginTreeWrite and EndTreeWrite, and BeginAVWrite
and EndAVWrite, and dropping an entry raises it too. Every entry carries
the version noted before its source was read, and attribute entries
carry their page's id. The caches' get functions serve an entry only if
nothing raised its page or database since, so every lookup is checked,
and an attribute entry for another page, or with none, is a miss. A
fill notes the version before its first lookup and stores with it; the
version is an argument of every store, and the bare-key block attribute
store is gone. A write's end runs even if the write panics, and the
write stores only if nothing else raised its page or database while it
ran. Clearing one cache raises only that cache's floor, and the version
map remembers 32,768 ids, forgetting into hashed buckets that keep the
largest version, so forgetting never empties every cache.

A job keeps its attribute changes to itself. Renders and row sorts
inside the job read them from the job, setAttributeViewContextFilter and
the database-view operations that wrote pages in the middle of a job
change the job's copy, and after the commit writes each page it stores
that page's attributes with the write's version, then sends their
pushes; a failed commit caches nothing. Outside jobs, attributes enter
the cache only after the write they copy, SetBlockReminder caches
nothing, kanban and gallery renders cache nothing, and a database's
history copy never touches the live entry.

Still open: files written around these writers, as sync, a snapshot
checkout, a data.zip import and the file API write them, raise no
version yet, so a copy cached before them can be served.

The patch carries 39 Go tests, most through what SiYuan serves. The 22
written before the fix test the faults above: 19 fail without it on
v3.8.7 with patches 0001 to 0004, among them a page fill and a database
fill racing a save, a move, a restore, a failed commit, a job's
attributes read before its page is written and "+ New" binding a page;
2 guard what must stay; and 1 checks the version map's bound. The rest
check an attribute entry for another page, a database removed while a
fill is noted, overlapping writes, floors under concurrent raises, a
write that panics, and jobs, reads, renders and writers at once, with
no stale read.
